**Local vs global handling.** A controller can define `@ExceptionHandler` methods that apply only to that controller. A class annotated `@RestControllerAdvice` applies to all controllers and is the standard place for a consistent error format.

**Guaranteeing a standard code and message.**
1. Define an `enum ErrorCode(val code: Int, val message: String, val status: HttpStatus)` such as `USER_NOT_FOUND(123, "User not found", NOT_FOUND)`.
2. Create one base exception that requires an error code, and have every domain exception extend it, so you cannot throw a business exception without a code.
3. Write one global handler for the base type that builds a standard `ErrorResponse(code, message, traceId)`.
4. Add a catch-all `@ExceptionHandler(Exception::class)` returning a generic 500 code with a safe message and logging the details, so unexpected exceptions still follow the contract and never leak internals.
5. Extend `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` to map framework exceptions (validation errors, malformed JSON, 405) into the same format.
6. Add tests that fail if an exception type lacks a mapping.

    open class AppException(val error: ErrorCode) : RuntimeException(error.message)
    class UserNotFound : AppException(ErrorCode.USER_NOT_FOUND)

    @RestControllerAdvice
    class GlobalHandler {
      @ExceptionHandler(AppException::class)
      fun app(e: AppException) = ResponseEntity.status(e.error.status).body(ErrorResponse(e.error.code, e.error.message))
      @ExceptionHandler(Exception::class)
      fun other(e: Exception) = ResponseEntity.status(500).body(ErrorResponse(500, "Internal error"))
    }

**RuntimeException vs Exception.** Checked exceptions (extend `Exception`) must be declared or caught; Spring rolls back transactions on unchecked exceptions by default, so business exceptions are usually unchecked. Kotlin has no checked exceptions at all.

**Authorization methods.** Authentication proves who you are; authorization decides what you may do. Sessions: server-side state plus a cookie, easy to revoke. JWT: signed stateless token, scales across services, harder to revoke. OAuth 2.0: delegated authorization through tokens issued by an authorization server. Role or permission checks use `@PreAuthorize`.
